  "diagnosis": "E129 rejects both a context-lock bottleneck and a native-allocation-only explanation. Native vkAllocateDescriptorSets consumes about half of measured worker time, but the worker accounts for only about 78 microseconds of a roughly 269-microsecond client transaction. The dominant remaining interval is the synchronous cross-process publication/wakeup/scheduling round trip. Journal replay is measurable but secondary, and WSI remains below 2 ms per present.",
  "recommendation": "Remove the per-set round trip and the repeated native allocation together. Use DXVK descriptor-pool reset epochs to preallocate or recycle same-layout native descriptor sets in bounded service-owned batches, publish typed leases to the client without a per-allocation wake, and retain authoritative pool-reset invalidation plus a strict fallback. Do not spend the next gate tuning the context mutex or Activity presentation path.",
  "claim_boundary": "This one complete profiling run identifies phase shares and preserves the 2.0-FPS baseline. Client and worker counters cover slightly different start/end windows, so the 191-microsecond cross-process gap is approximate and is not assigned to a specific scheduler or futex syscall without paired timestamps.",
  "reuse": "The required deja query returned no indexed E129 implementation. E129 reused E116/E117 bounded summaries, E128's exact descriptor worker boundary, and the existing final-process BVB_FRAME_PROFILE selector."
